/-!
# Derived order and lattice structure

The order on a Boolean algebra is to be derived from its algebraic operations,
for example by `x ≤ y ↔ x ∧ y = x`.  Bounded distributive lattice structure is
therefore a theorem/construction of the algebraic theory, not part of the
foundational definition.

This separation is intentional: order-theoretic interoperability should be a
consequence of Boolean algebra, while intrinsic Boolean constructions remain
independent of a lattice-first presentation.
-/

